  "title": "Kourovka Notebook Problem 21.34",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "(Well-known problem). A group $G$ is a unique product group if, for any nonempty finite subsets $A,B$ of $G$, there exists an element of $G$ which can be written uniquely as $ab$ with $a\\in A$ and $b\\in B$. A group $G$ is locally invariant orderable if $G$ admits a partial order $<$ such that for all $g,h\\in G$ with $h\\ne 1$, we have either $gh>g$ or $gh^{-1}>g$. Does there exist a unique product group which is not locally invariant orderable?",
